#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189409 :tutorial/content "For a set of random variables $X_1, \\ldots, X_n,$ the **covariance matrix** $\\Sigma$ is defined as\n\n![](images/41/416ac73d7b76e9c62efead1278e7a2b47c00dd03a8af17df99f28aea99f3f7a0.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The $i$th diagonal element is $\\Sigma_{ii} = \\textrm{Var}[X_i].$\n\n- The element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i \\neq j$ is $\\Sigma_{ij} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_i, X_j].$\n\nNow, since $\\textrm{Cov}[X_i,X_j] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_j,X_i]$ for any pair of random variables $X_i, X_j$, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The covariance matrix is always symmetric. Moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "85ebd531-80d8-5d04-b494-4547bb506c5a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6488 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
